From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nsiproxy, netprofm: the Network Connectivity Status Indicator's active probes decide whether a network reaches the internet

A network's internet connectivity was its default route: a hotel's network
before sign-in, a router with its line down, a VM with no way out all showed
"Internet access" to every program (INetworkListManager, Windows.Networking.
Connectivity, the taskbar). Windows' Network Connectivity Status Indicator
asks instead, and so does nsiproxy now:

- each address family with a default route is probed when the network
  changes, and again every two minutes while it finds no internet: an HTTP
  GET of http://<ActiveWebProbeHost>/<ActiveWebProbePath> must answer
  ActiveWebProbeContent (NlaSvc\Parameters\Internet, Windows' values:
  www.msftconnecttest.com/connecttest.txt "Microsoft Connect Test",
  ipv6.msftconnecttest.com for IPv6; wine.inf writes them, administrators may
  change them). Nothing answering is no internet, another page or a redirect a
  captive portal. EnableActiveProbing 0, or the policy
  NetworkConnectivityStatusIndicator\NoActiveProbe 1, turn probing off, as on
  Windows; then the default route decides as before.
- the answer is kept beside the configuration (SgActiveProbeResultV4/V6:
  0 not probed, 1 none, 2 a portal, 3 the internet), written by the
  machine's nsiproxy; netprofm reads it in every program, a family without
  internet reaches only its local network, and it watches the key, so a
  running program's manager changes its connectivity and tells its sinks
  (ConnectivityChanged and kin) when a portal is signed in to or a line
  goes down.

Gate: test/ncsi-gate.sh (a private network namespace, the probe page from
test/ncsi-server.py, test/ncsi-probe.c holding one manager through the page,
a portal, the page, nothing, probing off). Mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_NCSI,
SG_MUTANT_NCSI_ALWAYS_INTERNET, SG_MUTANT_NLM_IGNORE_NCSI.
